{"duration_api_ms":123550,"stop_reason":"tool_use","session_id":"ca947095-56b4-4058-90af-dc411215b746","total_cost_usd":0.2781774,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":39064,"cache_read_input_tokens":49617,"output_tokens":11199,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":5590},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":39064,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2829,"cache_read_input_tokens":40077,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8527,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8527},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-sonnet-5":{"inputTokens":4,"outputTokens":11199,"cacheReadInputTokens":49617,"cacheCreationInputTokens":39064,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.2781774,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"thinkingTokens":5590,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":3,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"requirements\":[{\"id\":\"managed-service\",\"requirement\":\"Run inventory-worker as a managed, long-running background service on node1\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"OpenRC service /etc/init.d/inventory-worker was installed, enabled in the default runlevel (rc-update add), started, and confirmed 'started' both before and after a reboot with the worker process actively looping and appending to its log every ~5s.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-5e451a4a95184f19\",\"cmd-50b2cd42699242fb\",\"cmd-0c1e54f2cf3b4401\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\"]},{\"id\":\"write-confinement\",\"requirement\":\"Worker is confined so it writes only inside /var/lib/inventory\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Final wrapper mounts a tmpfs-backed rw overlay of '/', bind-remounts that view read-only, then re-exposes the real /var/lib/inventory as the only writable bind mount before chrooting and dropping to the unprivileged worker. Direct tests show writes elsewhere fail with 'Read-only file system' while writes inside /var/lib/inventory succeed, and the worker's own /proc/PID/mountinfo shows its root mount as 'ro,relatime' both pre- and post-restart. One later diagnostic (nsenter -m into the worker's mount namespace, touching /etc) unexpectedly succeeded, but this was a flawed probe: nsenter -m alone does not apply the process's chroot, so it wrote to the real host /etc rather than the sandboxed view, and the stray file was immediately removed. It does not contradict the direct in-namespace evidence of confinement.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-63e13e79fede4777\",\"cmd-6116b59f9cd54f79\",\"cmd-8ee032356e5c44fb\",\"cmd-50b2cd42699242fb\",\"cmd-0c1e54f2cf3b4401\",\"cmd-5aab66f9cd5a4139\",\"cmd-4ef93e3c3e25451d\"]},{\"id\":\"no-new-privs\",\"requirement\":\"Worker gains no new privileges\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Worker process is launched via setpriv --no-new-privs and runs as unprivileged uid/gid 103/104 with CapEff 0000000000000000; /proc/PID/status shows NoNewPrivs:1 both before and after the mandatory restart.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-5e451a4a95184f19\",\"cmd-8ee032356e5c44fb\",\"cmd-50b2cd42699242fb\",\"cmd-0c1e54f2cf3b4401\"]},{\"id\":\"restart-evidence-protocol\",\"requirement\":\"(Independent) provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol executed and coherent for node1\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Pre-restart baseline boot_id (7b162ed4...) with healthy service/process/confinement state was captured, a backgrounded reboot was acknowledged, connection loss confirmed the node went down, a new boot_id (18690a58...) was observed post-recovery, and post-restart checks show the service auto-started, the worker process running under a new PID, the log persisted and continued appending, and all confinement flags (ro root, CapEff 0, NoNewPrivs 1, unprivileged uid/gid) intact. The fixed lifecycle boot-identity snapshots (before/after-executor) corroborate the same boot_id transition.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-50b2cd42699242fb\",\"cmd-3acd485743264e91\",\"cmd-2b21eb21e65945d8\",\"cmd-43dff021d1924fa4\",\"cmd-9df4aeb0d7434472\",\"cmd-0c1e54f2cf3b4401\",\"global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\"]}],\"confidence\":0.85,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.65,\"summary\":\"The executor iterated through several failed sandboxing approaches (direct remount,ro hitting EBUSY on the ext4 root; overlay mounts with wrong syntax) before landing on the working tmpfs-overlay + bind-remount-ro technique used in the final service (cmd-11d07a929582473b rc=1, cmd-1e2226fa75464743 rc=32 leaving the service in a 'crashed' state until fixed, cmd-b89aac9fc66c42d3, cmd-0c49ff804ad64ca5, cmd-8f0f48e49b5d4eed, cmd-e656f3f6545e47bc, cmd-f69be2e5710a4859). These were bounded, low-risk discovery steps that directly informed and fed into the final working configuration rather than being abandoned dead ends, and associated test mounts/dirs under /run were subsequently torn down (cmd-6116b59f9cd54f79, cmd-63e13e79fede4777, cmd-23a17c2740ae4e75 confirms 'no leftover mounts'). One diagnostic command (cmd-5aab66f9cd5a4139) used nsenter incorrectly and stray-wrote /etc/should-fail-write on the real host filesystem; this residue was identified and removed in the very next command (cmd-4ef93e3c3e25451d). Remaining unreverted residue: scratch setup scripts (/tmp/setup-inventory-worker.sh, -2.sh, -3.sh) and /tmp/reboot.log are still present in the after-executor temp-files snapshot -- low-risk but unnecessary leftover files that were never cleaned up.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-11d07a929582473b\",\"cmd-1e2226fa75464743\",\"cmd-b89aac9fc66c42d3\",\"cmd-0c49ff804ad64ca5\",\"cmd-8f0f48e49b5d4eed\",\"cmd-e656f3f6545e47bc\",\"cmd-f69be2e5710a4859\",\"cmd-6116b59f9cd54f79\",\"cmd-63e13e79fede4777\",\"cmd-23a17c2740ae4e75\",\"cmd-5aab66f9cd5a4139\",\"cmd-4ef93e3c3e25451d\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All three material outcomes of the task contract are satisfied with direct evidence: inventory-worker runs as an OpenRC-managed, boot-enabled, long-running service on node1; it is confined via a read-only overlay root with only /var/lib/inventory bind-mounted writable (writes elsewhere fail, writes inside succeed, confirmed via the worker's own mountinfo); and it runs with NoNewPrivs=1 and CapEff=0 under an unprivileged uid/gid. The independent restart-evidence protocol is also fully and coherently demonstrated: healthy pre-restart baseline, acknowledged reboot, confirmed boot_id change, and full post-restart recovery of service, data, and confinement state, corroborated by the fixed lifecycle boot-identity snapshots. Operational hygiene is moderate: the path to the working sandbox design involved several failed mount experiments (expected iteration, mostly cleaned up) plus one flawed nsenter diagnostic whose stray file was promptly removed, and a small amount of leftover scratch script residue in /tmp that was never cleaned up.\",\"limitations\":[\"The nsenter-based root-bypass probe (cmd-5aab66f9cd5a4139) was methodologically flawed (it did not apply the process's chroot) so it neither confirms nor refutes whether a colluding root actor joining the worker's mount namespace could bypass the read-only confinement; this scenario is outside the literal task contract but is noted as an evidence gap.\",\"Leftover /tmp scratch files (setup-inventory-worker*.sh, reboot.log) remain on node1 after the task; low risk but not cleaned up.\",\"Systemd-oriented lifecycle checks (failed-services) were unsupported on this OpenRC/Alpine node, so that portion of the fixed lifecycle snapshot provides no signal either way.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"managed-service","requirement":"Run inventory-worker as a managed, long-running background service on node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"OpenRC service /etc/init.d/inventory-worker was installed, enabled in the default runlevel (rc-update add), started, and confirmed 'started' both before and after a reboot with the worker process actively looping and appending to its log every ~5s.","evidence_ids":["cmd-5e451a4a95184f19","cmd-50b2cd42699242fb","cmd-0c1e54f2cf3b4401","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]},{"id":"write-confinement","requirement":"Worker is confined so it writes only inside /var/lib/inventory","status":"satisfied","summary":"Final wrapper mounts a tmpfs-backed rw overlay of '/', bind-remounts that view read-only, then re-exposes the real /var/lib/inventory as the only writable bind mount before chrooting and dropping to the unprivileged worker. Direct tests show writes elsewhere fail with 'Read-only file system' while writes inside /var/lib/inventory succeed, and the worker's own /proc/PID/mountinfo shows its root mount as 'ro,relatime' both pre- and post-restart. One later diagnostic (nsenter -m into the worker's mount namespace, touching /etc) unexpectedly succeeded, but this was a flawed probe: nsenter -m alone does not apply the process's chroot, so it wrote to the real host /etc rather than the sandboxed view, and the stray file was immediately removed. It does not contradict the direct in-namespace evidence of confinement.","evidence_ids":["cmd-63e13e79fede4777","cmd-6116b59f9cd54f79","cmd-8ee032356e5c44fb","cmd-50b2cd42699242fb","cmd-0c1e54f2cf3b4401","cmd-5aab66f9cd5a4139","cmd-4ef93e3c3e25451d"]},{"id":"no-new-privs","requirement":"Worker gains no new privileges","status":"satisfied","summary":"Worker process is launched via setpriv --no-new-privs and runs as unprivileged uid/gid 103/104 with CapEff 0000000000000000; /proc/PID/status shows NoNewPrivs:1 both before and after the mandatory restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-5e451a4a95184f19","cmd-8ee032356e5c44fb","cmd-50b2cd42699242fb","cmd-0c1e54f2cf3b4401"]},{"id":"restart-evidence-protocol","requirement":"(Independent) provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol executed and coherent for node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"Pre-restart baseline boot_id (7b162ed4...) with healthy service/process/confinement state was captured, a backgrounded reboot was acknowledged, connection loss confirmed the node went down, a new boot_id (18690a58...) was observed post-recovery, and post-restart checks show the service auto-started, the worker process running under a new PID, the log persisted and continued appending, and all confinement flags (ro root, CapEff 0, NoNewPrivs 1, unprivileged uid/gid) intact. The fixed lifecycle boot-identity snapshots (before/after-executor) corroborate the same boot_id transition.","evidence_ids":["cmd-50b2cd42699242fb","cmd-3acd485743264e91","cmd-2b21eb21e65945d8","cmd-43dff021d1924fa4","cmd-9df4aeb0d7434472","cmd-0c1e54f2cf3b4401","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]}],"confidence":0.85,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.65,"summary":"The executor iterated through several failed sandboxing approaches (direct remount,ro hitting EBUSY on the ext4 root; overlay mounts with wrong syntax) before landing on the working tmpfs-overlay + bind-remount-ro technique used in the final service (cmd-11d07a929582473b rc=1, cmd-1e2226fa75464743 rc=32 leaving the service in a 'crashed' state until fixed, cmd-b89aac9fc66c42d3, cmd-0c49ff804ad64ca5, cmd-8f0f48e49b5d4eed, cmd-e656f3f6545e47bc, cmd-f69be2e5710a4859). These were bounded, low-risk discovery steps that directly informed and fed into the final working configuration rather than being abandoned dead ends, and associated test mounts/dirs under /run were subsequently torn down (cmd-6116b59f9cd54f79, cmd-63e13e79fede4777, cmd-23a17c2740ae4e75 confirms 'no leftover mounts'). One diagnostic command (cmd-5aab66f9cd5a4139) used nsenter incorrectly and stray-wrote /etc/should-fail-write on the real host filesystem; this residue was identified and removed in the very next command (cmd-4ef93e3c3e25451d). Remaining unreverted residue: scratch setup scripts (/tmp/setup-inventory-worker.sh, -2.sh, -3.sh) and /tmp/reboot.log are still present in the after-executor temp-files snapshot -- low-risk but unnecessary leftover files that were never cleaned up.","evidence_ids":["cmd-11d07a929582473b","cmd-1e2226fa75464743","cmd-b89aac9fc66c42d3","cmd-0c49ff804ad64ca5","cmd-8f0f48e49b5d4eed","cmd-e656f3f6545e47bc","cmd-f69be2e5710a4859","cmd-6116b59f9cd54f79","cmd-63e13e79fede4777","cmd-23a17c2740ae4e75","cmd-5aab66f9cd5a4139","cmd-4ef93e3c3e25451d","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All three material outcomes of the task contract are satisfied with direct evidence: inventory-worker runs as an OpenRC-managed, boot-enabled, long-running service on node1; it is confined via a read-only overlay root with only /var/lib/inventory bind-mounted writable (writes elsewhere fail, writes inside succeed, confirmed via the worker's own mountinfo); and it runs with NoNewPrivs=1 and CapEff=0 under an unprivileged uid/gid. The independent restart-evidence protocol is also fully and coherently demonstrated: healthy pre-restart baseline, acknowledged reboot, confirmed boot_id change, and full post-restart recovery of service, data, and confinement state, corroborated by the fixed lifecycle boot-identity snapshots. Operational hygiene is moderate: the path to the working sandbox design involved several failed mount experiments (expected iteration, mostly cleaned up) plus one flawed nsenter diagnostic whose stray file was promptly removed, and a small amount of leftover scratch script residue in /tmp that was never cleaned up.","limitations":["The nsenter-based root-bypass probe (cmd-5aab66f9cd5a4139) was methodologically flawed (it did not apply the process's chroot) so it neither confirms nor refutes whether a colluding root actor joining the worker's mount namespace could bypass the read-only confinement; this scenario is outside the literal task contract but is noted as an evidence gap.","Leftover /tmp scratch files (setup-inventory-worker*.sh, reboot.log) remain on node1 after the task; low risk but not cleaned up.","Systemd-oriented lifecycle checks (failed-services) were unsupported on this OpenRC/Alpine node, so that portion of the fixed lifecycle snapshot provides no signal either way."]},"ttft_ms":66693,"type":"result","duration_ms":123761,"uuid":"6b3c1e97-8442-42b0-90c4-843a557b76d2","ttft_stream_ms":1317,"time_to_request_ms":200,"first_content_frame_ms":1317,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
